Algorithm Lab Report:
Methodology:
Tested Selection, Insertion, Merge, and Array sort using randomly generated integer
arrays. Each input size used 5 untimed warm-up runs and 12 timed trials in order to get
more reliable results. The execute() call was timed using System.nanoTime(). Selection and
Insertion sort used a range of sizes from 500 - 8,000 due to their O(n^2) growth becomes
big quickly. Merge and Arrays sort used sizes 10,000 - 640,000 cause their expected O(n\log
n) growth allows larger inputs.
Plots:
Created one execution-time plot for each algorithm using input size on the x-axis
and the median execution time on the y-axis. Selection sort compared with (n^2) fitted
curve. Insertion sort compared with (n^2) fitted curve. Merge sort compared with (n\log n)
fitted curve. Arrays sort compared with (n\log n) fitted curve.
Model-fit comparison:
• Selection sort:
o Expected model: n^2
o Best model: n^2
o MAPE: 28.5743%
o R^2: 0.834792
• Insertion sort:
o Expected model: n^2
o Best model: n\log n
o MAPE: 39.8778%
o R^2: 0.843633
• Merge sort:
o Expected model: n\log n
o Best model: n\log n
o MAPE: 2.0402%
o R^2: 0.999469
• Arrays sort:
o Expected model: n\log n
o Best model: n
o MAPE: 10.1355%
o R^2: 0.996054
Reflection:
My results pretty much matched what I expected, but not completely. Selection sort
matched the expected O(n^2) growth, and Merge sort matched O(n\log n) really well. Merge
sort had the best fit overall, with a MAPE of about 2.04% and an R^2 very close to 1. Insertion
sort was surprising because n\log n fit better than n^2, and Arrays sort matched a linear
model better than n\log n for the input sizes I tested. I think some of these differences
happened because Big-O describes how an algorithm grows in general, not how long it will
take on one computer. Things like JVM warm-up, JIT compilation, cache behavior, garbage
collection, and other programs running in the background can affect the timing results.
Even though I used warm-up runs and repeated trials, there was still some variation in the
measurements. If I had more time, I would test more input sizes and probably use more
trials for each size. I would also try more warm-up runs and probably use a tool like JMH to
get more accurate timing results. Overall, I think that would make the results more
consistent and make it easier to compare them to the theoretical growth rates.
